@page "/Account/Manage/PersonalData"

@inject IdentityUserAccessor UserAccessor

<PageTitle>Personal data - Meridian Dental Surgery</PageTitle>

<StatusMessage />

<div class="card-ds mb-3">
    <div class="card-ds-header">
        <h3>Your personal data</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="card-ds-body">
        <p class="settings-lede">
            This is about <strong>you as a member of staff</strong>, not about patients.
            It holds what the practice recorded when your account was issued: your
            name, email address, contact number and job title.
        </p>

        <p class="text-muted settings-note">
            A patient asking for their own record is a different request, answered
            from the patient's file rather than from here.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card-ds-footer">
        <form action="Account/Manage/DownloadPersonalData" method="post">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <button class="btn btn-sm btn-primary" type="submit">
                <Icon Name="download" Size="14" /> Download my data
            </button>
        </form>
        <span class="text-faint" style="font-size:.78rem;">Downloads as a JSON file.</span>
    </div>
</div>

@*
    The scaffolded "delete my account" control has been removed rather than
    hidden. A login here is the author of clinical notes, prescriptions and
    financial entries, and deleting it would break the attribution those records
    depend on — the audit trail is append-only by design. Withdrawing someone's
    access is a deactivation, carried out by an administrator, which stops the
    account working while leaving what it signed intact.

    Saying so is better than silence: someone who came looking for a delete
    button needs to know what to do instead, not to conclude the page is broken.
*@
<div class="card-ds">
    <div class="card-ds-header">
        <h3>Closing your account</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="card-ds-body">
        <p class="settings-lede">
            An account cannot be deleted from here, and that is deliberate. Clinical
            notes, prescriptions and ledger entries you have signed must keep naming
            their author, or the record stops being evidence of who did what.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted settings-note">
            When you leave, a practice administrator deactivates the account instead.
            It stops working immediately — sign-in is refused and any open session
            ends — while everything you signed stays attributed to you.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
